Below is a **self‑contained console application** that shows how the built‑in generic collection types in .NET are used, and even introduces a tiny custom generic type.  
The program demonstrates:

* `List<T>` – a growable ordered collection.  
* `Dictionary<TKey,TValue>` – a key/value map.  
* `HashSet<T>` – a collection that automatically removes duplicates.  
* A generic method that can iterate over any collection that implements `IEnumerable<T>`.  
* A simple generic class `Pair<T1,T2>` that can hold two unrelated values.

```csharp
// GenericCollectionsDemo.cs
using System;
using System.Collections.Generic;

namespace GenericCollectionsDemo
{
    // --------------------------------------------------------------------
    // A tiny generic class that holds two unrelated values.
    // --------------------------------------------------------------------
    public class Pair<T1, T2>
    {
        public T1 First  { get; set; }
        public T2 Second { get; set; }

        public Pair(T1 first, T2 second)
        {
            First  = first;
            Second = second;
        }

        public override string ToString() =>
            $"({First}, {Second})";
    }

    // --------------------------------------------------------------------
    // Generic helper – can print any IEnumerable<T>.
    // --------------------------------------------------------------------
    public static class CollectionUtils
    {
        public static void PrintCollection<T>(IEnumerable<T> collection, string title = "")
        {
            if (!string.IsNullOrEmpty(title))
                Console.WriteLine(title);

            foreach (T item in collection)
                Console.WriteLine($"  {item}");

            Console.WriteLine();   // blank line for readability
        }
    }

    // --------------------------------------------------------------------
    // The entry point.
    // --------------------------------------------------------------------
    class Program
    {
        static void Main()
        {
            // 1. List<int> – an ordered list of integers.
            List<int> numbers = new List<int> { 1, 2, 3 };
            numbers.Add(4);                     // add one more
            numbers.Insert(0, 0);                // insert at beginning
            CollectionUtils.PrintCollection(numbers, "List<int> numbers:");

            // 2. Dictionary<string, int> – map a name to an age.
            Dictionary<string, int> ages = new Dictionary<string, int>
            {
                { "Alice", 30 },
                { "Bob",   25 },
                { "Carol", 28 }
            };
            // Change a value
            ages["Alice"] = 31;
            // Iterate over key/value pairs
            CollectionUtils.PrintCollection(ages, "Dictionary<string,int> ages:");

            // 3. HashSet<string> – a set that keeps unique items.
            HashSet<string> fruits = new HashSet<string>();
            fruits.Add("Apple");
            fruits.Add("Banana");
            fruits.Add("Apple");      // duplicate – ignored
            CollectionUtils.PrintCollection(fruits, "HashSet<string> fruits:");

            // 4. Generic class Pair<T1,T2>
            Pair<string, double> weight = new Pair<string, double>("Pencil", 0.05);
            Console.WriteLine($"Pair example: {weight}\n");

            // 5. A generic method that can consume any IEnumerable<T>.
            //    We'll use it on a List<string> and a Dictionary<int,string> keys.
            List<string> colors = new List<string> { "Red", "Green", "Blue" };
            CollectionUtils.PrintCollection(colors, "List<string> colors:");

            CollectionUtils.PrintCollection(ages.Keys, "Dictionary keys (string)"); // keys are IEnumerable<string>
            CollectionUtils.PrintCollection(ages.Values, "Dictionary values (int)"); // values are IEnumerable<int>
        }
    }
}
```

### What this code demonstrates

| Feature | What it shows |
|---------|---------------|
| `List<int>` | Adding, inserting, and iterating over a generic list. |
| `Dictionary<string,int>` | Key/value pairing, updating a value, iterating with `KeyValuePair<TKey,TValue>`. |
| `HashSet<string>` | Automatic duplicate elimination, simple iteration. |
| `Pair<T1,T2>` | Creating a reusable generic class that can hold two different types. |
| `PrintCollection<T>` | A generic method that accepts any `IEnumerable<T>` (List, Dictionary keys/values, etc.). |

Run the program (e.g. `dotnet run`) and you’ll see output similar to:

```
List<int> numbers:
  0
  1
  2
  3
  4

Dictionary<string,int> ages:
  Alice: 31
  Bob: 25
  Carol: 28

HashSet<string> fruits:
  Banana
  Apple

Pair example: (Pencil, 0.05)

List<string> colors:
  Red
  Green
  Blue

Dictionary keys (string):
  Alice
  Bob
  Carol

Dictionary values (int):
  31
  25
  28
```

Feel free to modify the collections, add more generic types, or replace the console output with something else. The example gives a solid foundation for working with generic collections in C#.
